Null hypothesis (H_0): μ = 0.0

Alternative hypothesis (H_A): μ > 0.0

sample mean for sample 1 = 2.3

standard deviation for sample 1 = 1.7

sample size for sample 1 = 3

alpha level = 0.5

t statistic = 2.343362857299069

degrees of freedom = 2

p value = 0.072

Assuming the null hypothesis is true (p = 0), there is approximately a 0.072 probability of obtaining a sample statistic equal to or greater than the one observed in our sample purely by chance.

Because the p value (0.072) is less than 0.5, we successfully reject the null and have convincing evidence that the alternate hypothesis (μ > 0) is true.